<h2 id="start-this-month">How to start a side hustle in Louisville this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Choose one hustle.</strong> Match it to the hours you can protect every week, not the ones you hope to find.</li>
  <li><strong>Register with the Revenue Commission.</strong> Self-employed Louisville residents owe the occupational license tax, so set up your account early and avoid surprise penalties.</li>
  <li><strong>Set a monthly target.</strong> Use the <a href="/tools/income-calculator/">income calculator</a> to work backward to hours per week.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed.</strong> Sign up for the apps, or post on Nextdoor and neighborhood Facebook groups and set up a free Google Business Profile.</li>
  <li><strong>Book Derby early.</strong> Line up cleaning, co-hosting, parking and staffing gigs for late April and early May by February.</li>
  <li><strong>Track everything.</strong> Separate bank account, mileage log and receipts from day one.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="kentucky-taxes">Kentucky and Louisville taxes for side income</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> Kentucky has a flat individual income tax of 3.5% for 2026, down from 4% in 2025.</li>
  <li><strong>Louisville Metro occupational license tax:</strong> residents pay 2.2% of net profit from self-employment and non-residents who work in Louisville pay 1.45%. Net profits are reported on the annual OL-3 return to the Louisville Metro Revenue Commission, and people with larger tax bills must make quarterly estimated payments.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit plus federal income tax. Estimate your set-aside with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> Kentucky's 6% sales tax applies to physical goods and, after recent law changes, a growing list of services. Check whether yours is covered before you set prices.</li>
  <li><strong>Short-term rentals:</strong> require registration with Louisville Metro and are subject to lodging taxes.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Live in Southern Indiana but hustle in Louisville? You'll likely owe the non-resident occupational rate on Louisville earnings plus Indiana tax. Keep your income records separated by where the work happened.</div>

